{
  "version": 1,
  "comments": {
    "c1": {
      "anchor": "i18",
      "kind": "item",
      "label": "Spell UI gaps the API examples keep CSS for: an image's crop, and capitals on headers, buttons and labels",
      "excerpt": "Spell UI gaps the API examples keep CSS for: an image's crop, and capitals on headers, buttons and labels",
      "text": "Add aspect ratio and crop to image\nFix ui-text bits",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-06T23:40:31.994Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-07T00:17:35.684Z",
          "html": "<p>Done (I18).  <code>&lt;ui-image&gt;</code> takes two new attributes, and every element's capitals now work:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>\n    <code>aspect-ratio=\"2:3\"</code> (or <code>16/9</code>, <code>1</code>):  the picture's shape, the whole picture inside it.\n  </li>\n  <li>\n    <code>crop</code>:  it fills that shape, cut to fit, keeping the middle;  <code>crop=\"top\"</code>, <code>\"top left\"</code> ... keep that part.\n    It's a class word, as Fomantic writes a variation (<code>ui top cropped image</code>).\n  </li>\n  <li>\n    The ui-text bits:  <code>font=\"title-caps\"</code> and <code>font=\"all-caps\"</code> already worked on <code>&lt;ui-text&gt;</code>, but a header's, a button's and a label's own style set the case back to normal.\n    Now they reach the words of those three too.\n  </li>\n  <li>\n    The examples:  Public Servers' styles.css is gone (its covers and artworks are <code>aspect-ratio crop</code>);\n    PokéSpell's names use <code>font=\"title-caps\"</code> and its pictures <code>aspect-ratio=\"1\"</code>;\n    Star Wars' titles and buttons use <code>font=\"all-caps\"</code>, and its photos <code>crop=\"top\"</code>.\n  </li>\n  <li>The image and header docs pages have an example of each.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Not done:  Spell has no <code>as title case</code> for text itself, only the look.</p>"
        }
      ],
      "claim": {
        "by": "owenoak",
        "agent": "claude",
        "at": "2026-11-06T23:50:47.501Z"
      }
    },
    "c2": {
      "anchor": "i19",
      "kind": "item",
      "label": "P30: a <for> can't sit inside an element, so a list of cards can't share a <ui-cards> or <ui-container> with another card",
      "excerpt": "P30: a <for> can't sit inside an element, so a list of cards can't share a <ui-cards> or <ui-container> with another card",
      "text": "fix it",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-06T23:40:53.727Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-07T00:25:52.187Z",
          "html": "<p>Fixed (I19).  A <code>&lt;for&gt;</code> can now sit inside an element, among its other children:  it reads exactly as it does on a line of its own, its attributes, <code>&lt;empty&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;failed&gt;</code> the same.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Recipe Box, Caja de Recetas and Family Cookbook draw the new recipe's card and a card per recipe in ONE <code>&lt;cards&gt;</code>, inside a <code>&lt;container&gt;</code>.</li>\n  <li>Statusphere's statuses are back inline in its <code>&lt;container text&gt;</code>;  its <code>&lt;draw \"statuses\"&gt;</code> is gone.</li>\n</ul>"
        }
      ]
    },
    "c3": {
      "anchor": "i20",
      "kind": "item",
      "label": "P30: add the new recipe to the recipes reads as a call of the action named add the new recipe",
      "excerpt": "P30: add the new recipe to the recipes reads as a call of the action named add the new recipe",
      "text": "fix",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-06T23:41:21.003Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-07T00:25:53.039Z",
          "html": "<p>Fixed (I20).  A line that starts with an action's name but goes on past it, <code>add the new recipe to the recipes</code>, now reads as the whole sentence (adding to the list), not as a call of <code>add the new recipe</code> with words left over.  The three recipe examples say it that way again;  <code>add the new recipe</code> alone still calls the action.</p>"
        }
      ]
    }
  },
  "on": {}
}
